From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Barnes <hello@jonbarnes.dev>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 20:45:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Water is not foliage, and the normal is what says so

Patch 0019 of the renderer series (OoT: Recompiled).

Four frames from the user named this between them. The default and the wider probe put a shadow ON
the water's surface and showed no reflections; the rung that reclassifies without casting put the
shadow on the bed under the water, correctly, and still showed no reflections; the old rule showed
real reflections and no foliage shadows.

All four follow from one thing: water is being reclassified as solid. Solid water is what the
primary ray stops at, so a shadow lands on its surface instead of passing through to the bed. And
the reflections cannot work at all while it is solid, because the probe looks for a translucent
surface IN FRONT of the opaque hit and water IS that hit, which is why widening the probe's mask
found nothing and was aimed at the wrong thing entirely.

So water must stay as it was and foliage must not, and on the CPU the two are identical: both in
the translucent z mode, both reading depth, neither writing it, both with plenty of triangles.
What differs is the NORMAL, which is unknown when an instance is classified and known at the hit.
The water probe has relied on exactly that since it was written.

A horizontal face of a depthless translucent draw is now passed through at the hit, by both the
surface ray and the shadow ray so the two agree, with the same threshold the probe uses. Water
behaves as it did before any of this work, the probe finds it again, and foliage is untouched.

On a rung, because the user asked for the reflection work to be cycled rather than applied and
because the default is the state he confirmed.

With the ray tracing row Off none of this runs.
---
diff --git a/src/shaders/Raytracing.hlsl b/src/shaders/Raytracing.hlsl
--- a/src/shaders/Raytracing.hlsl
+++ b/src/shaders/Raytracing.hlsl
@@ -64,6 +64,13 @@ float alphaCoverage(uint instanceId, uint primitiveIndex, float2 barycentrics, b
 // because the primary ray's cut-out compare needs it long before those buffers exist.
 bool hitWritesDepth(int instanceId);
 
+// Is this triangle of this instance a HORIZONTAL face of a draw in the translucent z mode that
+// never writes depth? That is water in this game, and it is the one thing the classification
+// cannot tell from foliage, because the two are identical in everything the CPU can see: both
+// translucent, both reading depth, neither writing it, both plenty of triangles. They differ in
+// their NORMAL, which is not known when an instance is classified and is known here.
+bool hitIsWaterLike(uint instanceId, uint primitiveIndex);
+
 // IS A LADDER RUNG BELOW `rung` SELECTED (patch 0017)? The foliage work was settled on the
 // Experiment row and then made the DEFAULT, because a fix nobody can find is not shipped: that
 // row is for debugging and no player will ever cycle to its twenty-fifth option.
@@ -1372,6 +1379,27 @@ bool hitWritesDepth(int instanceId) {
     return true;
 }
 
+bool hitIsWaterLike(uint instanceId, uint primitiveIndex) {
+    const RenderIndices ri = instanceRenderIndices[instanceId];
+    const RenderParams rp = DynamicRenderParams[ri.instanceIndex];
+    const OtherMode otherMode = { rp.omL, rp.omH };
+    if ((otherMode.zMode() != ZMODE_XLU) || otherMode.zUpd()) {
+        return false;
+    }
+    const uint3 indices = indexBuffer.Load3((ri.faceIndicesStart + primitiveIndex * 3) * 4);
+    const float3 p0 = asfloat(posBuffer.Load3(indices.x * 16));
+    const float3 p1 = asfloat(posBuffer.Load3(indices.y * 16));
+    const float3 p2 = asfloat(posBuffer.Load3(indices.z * 16));
+    const float3 n = cross(p1 - p0, p2 - p0);
+    const float len2 = dot(n, n);
+    if (len2 <= 0.0f) {
+        return false;
+    }
+    // The same threshold the water probe has used since it was written, so the two agree about
+    // what water is rather than each having an opinion.
+    return abs(n.y * rsqrt(len2)) > 0.9f;
+}
+
 float alphaCoverage(uint instanceId, uint primitiveIndex, float2 barycentrics, bool primary) {
     const RenderIndices ri = instanceRenderIndices[instanceId];
     const RenderParams rp = DynamicRenderParams[ri.instanceIndex];
@@ -1515,6 +1543,21 @@ float alphaCoverage(uint instanceId, uint primitiveIndex, float2 barycentrics, b
 
 [shader("anyhit")]
 void SurfaceAnyHit(inout SurfacePayload payload, in BuiltInTriangleIntersectionAttributes attributes) {
+    // WATER IS NOT A SURFACE THESE RAYS STOP AT (patch 0019, rung 30). The foliage rule cannot
+    // tell water from foliage, because on the CPU they are identical, so it reclassifies both;
+    // and a solid water surface is what the user saw as a shadow lying ON the water instead of on
+    // the bed, and as reflections disappearing entirely. The reflections cannot work while water
+    // is solid at all: the probe looks for a translucent surface IN FRONT of the opaque hit, and
+    // when water IS that hit there is nothing in front of it, which no mask can repair.
+    //
+    // Decided here because the normal is known here and is not known when an instance is
+    // classified. A horizontal face of a depthless translucent draw is passed through exactly as
+    // it was before the foliage work, so the primary reaches the bed, the shadow lands on the bed,
+    // and the probe finds the water in front of it. A vertical one is foliage and is unaffected.
+    if (hitIsWaterLike(InstanceID(), PrimitiveIndex()) && (RtParams.tuneExperiment.x == 30.0f)) {
+        IgnoreHit();
+        return;
+    }
     // Nothing of the surface in this pixel: the ray goes on. Anything of it: the hit stands, and
     // the closest hit records how much (a partial cover far away weights the shading).
     if (alphaCoverage(InstanceID(), PrimitiveIndex(), attributes.barycentrics, true) <= 0.0f) {
@@ -1534,6 +1577,12 @@ void SurfaceMiss(inout SurfacePayload payload) {
 
 [shader("anyhit")]
 void ShadowAnyHit(inout ShadowPayload payload, in BuiltInTriangleIntersectionAttributes attributes) {
+    // Water casts nothing either (patch 0019, rung 30), for the same reason and so that the two
+    // rays agree about what water is.
+    if (hitIsWaterLike(InstanceID(), PrimitiveIndex()) && (RtParams.tuneExperiment.x == 30.0f)) {
+        IgnoreHit();
+        return;
+    }
     // A transparent texel casts no shadow (patch 0008): a tree line's quad shadowed as a slab.
     if (alphaCoverage(InstanceID(), PrimitiveIndex(), attributes.barycentrics, false) <= 0.0f) {
         IgnoreHit();
